<h2 id="materials-heading">Conveyor Belt Materials</h2>
<h3>PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride)</h3>
<p>The most common belt material for general-purpose packaging conveyors. PVC belts are economical, available in many surface finishes (smooth, rough-top, pattern-top), and suitable for temperatures from 14°F to 180°F. Standard PVC is not FDA-approved for direct food contact. Food-grade PVC variants are available but are being replaced by PU in food applications.</p>
<h3>PU (Polyurethane)</h3>
<p>Preferred for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage applications</a> because PU is FDA-compliant, resistant to oils and fats, and easier to clean than PVC. PU belts offer better abrasion resistance and longer service life than PVC but cost more. Temperature range is similar to PVC. White and blue PU is standard for food lines (blue for visibility in case of belt fragments).</p>
<h3>Modular Plastic</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> consist of interlocking plastic modules joined by hinge pins. Materials include polypropylene (PP), acetal (POM), and polyethylene (PE), all available in FDA-compliant grades. Modular belts excel in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary washdown environments</a> because individual modules can be removed for cleaning or replacement. Intralox and Habasit are leading modular belt manufacturers.</p>
<h3>Wire Mesh / Metal</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> use woven or welded metal wire for high-temperature applications (baking, cooling, heat treatment) and applications requiring airflow through the belt. Stainless steel mesh handles temperatures to 2000°F+ depending on alloy. Carbon steel is lower cost for non-corrosive, non-food applications. Wire mesh belts are also used for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling conveyors</a> where airflow is needed.</p>
<h3>Specialty Materials</h3>
<p>Specialty belt materials include silicone (high-temperature, non-stick), Teflon/PTFE coating (release properties), anti-static formulations, metal-detectable plastics, and reinforced rubber for heavy-duty bulk handling. Material selection depends on product contact requirements, temperature, chemical exposure, and regulatory compliance (FDA, USDA, 3-A).</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Belt Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Primary Packaging</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors transport products through primary packaging operations: filling, sealing, wrapping, and labeling. Flat belts provide stable, consistent product positioning for accurate filling and labeling. Precision belt conveyors with servo drives support <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing motions</a> for intermittent packaging machines.</p>
<h3>Incline and Decline Transport</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors handle <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline and decline transport</a> that roller conveyors cannot. Standard flat belts handle up to 15-20° inclines depending on product friction. <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">Cleated belts</a> enable steeper inclines up to 90° (vertical). Rough-top and high-grip belt surfaces extend incline capability without cleats.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage plants</a> use belt conveyors for raw ingredient handling, product transport between processing steps, and finished goods packaging. <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> dominate wet environments. <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> carry products through <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">bakery ovens</a>, fryers, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling tunnels</a>.</p>
<h3>Small Parts and Components</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors transport small parts, components, and assemblies that would fall between roller conveyor gaps. Flat belt conveyors with side <a href="/components/guide-rails/">guide rails</a> contain small items. <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">Vacuum belt conveyors</a> grip flat, lightweight items (labels, cartons, pouches) that standard friction cannot hold.</p>
<h3>Bags, Pouches, and Flexible Packaging</h3>
<p>Flexible packaging (bags, pouches, pillow packs) cannot travel on roller conveyors because they conform to roller gaps. Belt conveyors provide the continuous surface these products require. Gentle-handling belts with soft surfaces prevent puncturing or crushing delicate pouches.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Belt Conveyor</h2>
<p>Belt conveyor selection involves matching belt type, material, width, and speed to your product and application requirements.</p>
<p><strong>1. Product characteristics.</strong> Consider product weight (determines belt strength and motor sizing), dimensions (determines belt width), bottom surface (smooth, irregular, soft, rigid), temperature (hot products require heat-resistant belts), and sensitivity (fragile products need soft or cushioned belts).</p>
<p><strong>2. Environmental requirements.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Food contact</a> requires FDA-compliant materials. Washdown environments require sealed components and corrosion-resistant construction. High or low temperatures require appropriate belt materials. Chemical exposure requires compatible belt and frame materials.</p>
<p><strong>3. Incline angle.</strong> Calculate the incline angle from floor rise and horizontal run. Select belt type based on maximum incline capability: flat belt (15-20°), rough-top (25-30°), <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated</a> (up to 90°). Higher inclines require slower speeds.</p>
<p><strong>4. Speed and throughput.</strong> Calculate required belt speed from throughput and product spacing. Higher speeds require more robust belt construction, larger motors, and attention to product stability at load/unload points.</p>
<p><strong>5. Accumulation needs.</strong> Belt conveyors do not accumulate as easily as <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. If accumulation is needed, consider a belt conveyor with a <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">roller accumulation section</a>, or a modular plastic belt with low-friction top surface that allows slip accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>6. Cleaning and maintenance.</strong> Evaluate cleaning requirements and <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">maintenance access</a>. <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> are easiest to clean and repair (individual modules replace without removing the entire belt). Fabric belts may require more frequent replacement in abrasive or wet environments.</p>
