<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">conveyor manufacturers directory</a> for a comprehensive list.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A belt conveyor uses a continuous loop of material (the belt) stretched between two or more pulleys to transport products. The belt wraps around a head pulley (driven) and a tail pulley (idler), with the top surface carrying products and the bottom returning empty. Belt conveyors handle products of any shape, size, or bottom condition, including bags, pouches, and irregular shapes that <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a> cannot transport.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What are the different types of conveyor belts?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Common conveyor belt types include: <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">flat belt</a> (smooth surface for general transport), <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belt</a> (raised flights for inclines), <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular plastic belt</a> (interlocking modules for food and washdown), timing/positive drive belt (toothed for precise positioning), <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh belt</a> (open weave for high temperatures), and specialty belts (vacuum, magnetic, rough-top).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between a belt conveyor and a roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors use a continuous belt surface and can transport products of any shape or bottom condition. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a> use cylindrical rollers and require flat, rigid product bottoms spanning at least 3 rollers. Belt conveyors excel at <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">inclines</a>, handling bags and pouches, and transporting small items. Roller conveyors offer easier zone control, <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>, and lower friction for heavy flat-bottomed loads.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What belt conveyor is best for food applications?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belt conveyors</a> are preferred for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage applications</a> because they are easy to clean, resistant to water and chemicals, available in FDA-compliant materials, and suitable for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">washdown environments</a>. For dry food, FDA-compliant PU flat belts are also common. <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> are used for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">baking</a>, <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling</a>, and freezing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How fast can a belt conveyor run?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyor speeds typically range from 20 to 400 feet per minute (FPM) depending on the application. Light-duty <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging conveyors</a> commonly run at 50-150 FPM. High-speed sortation and distribution conveyors may reach 400-600 FPM. Maximum practical speed depends on belt type, product stability, and the ability to load and unload at the conveyor ends. <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> run slower to prevent product slippage.</p>

</div>
</div>
